course: everyday
lesson: 491
title: Rich families: the big house, the nanny and never seeing Dad
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Lottie lives in a big house in Surrey. Her dad works in finance in London and is hardly ever home before she's asleep. Her nanny, Gabi, picks her up every day, plaits her hair and knows her favourite biscuit. «Gabi knows me better than Dad does,» Lottie says one night, and it's true. She loves Gabi and misses Dad. Money can buy help, but it can't buy time with Dad, and grown-ups don't always notice that, so Lottie can tell him: «I miss you.»
explain: Rich kids have feelings like everyone else. In Los Angeles, Monty has a pool and a cinema room, and his friends love coming over, until he hears one kid say, «I only go to Monty's for the pool, he's mid, no cap» («mid» means boring, «no cap» means no lie). Monty is gutted. Real friends like you, not your pool. In New York, Clementine's parents miss her school play because of a work trip; they send flowers and a video call. She smiles on the call, then cries in the restroom. Money can't replace being there, and she can tell them what matters to her.
explain: Being rich can feel awkward too. In London, Anya mentions skiing in Switzerland and a girl says, «Stop flexing» («flexing» means showing off). Anya didn't mean to brag; she just didn't realise how different her holidays are. She can still share, and ask about other people's holidays too. In Sydney, Sienna gets a new iPhone and her mate Islay, whose family is on a tight budget, says, «Must be nice.» Sienna says, «It's not my fault,» and both feel awkward. Neither of them is to blame, and an honest chat can fix it.
explain: Some rich kids just want to fit in. A driver collects Arnav from school in a black car, and kids ask, «Is he a prince or what?» He'd rather walk home with his mates, and he can ask his parents if he sometimes may. In Cheshire, Imogen hears a mum at her marquee party (a party in a huge tent in the garden) mutter, «Must have cost a fortune,» and feels as if she's done something wrong. She hasn't: it's her party, and the best thing she can do is be a kind host. And in London, Felix's mate says, «You're spoilt, bruv.» Getting lots of stuff doesn't make you a bad person; saying thank you, sharing and being kind is what matters.
explain: Money can also mean missing out on ordinary things. Henry in Gloucestershire hears he's going to boarding school next year: he'll live at school in the week and come home at weekends. He's scared and doesn't want to leave his dog, and the wise move is to tell his parents how he feels and ask lots of questions. At a sleepover in Kent, Leo-Ray burns the toast because at home staff always made it, and everyone laughs; he laughs too, then asks his friend's mum to show him. Everyday skills are worth learning, whatever your house is like.
fact: Some rich families have parents who work very long hours; money can pay for help but not for time together.
fact: A nanny is a person paid to look after children at home; children often love their nanny, and that's fine.
fact: Telling a busy parent «I miss you» helps, because grown-ups don't always notice.
fact: Some kids visit a rich child only for their things; real friends like the person, not the pool.
fact: Children from rich families sometimes don't realise how different their life is, so others may hear bragging; sharing is fine if you also ask about others.
fact: Different amounts of money can make a friendship awkward, but neither friend is to blame, and talking honestly helps.
fact: Many rich kids just want to fit in, like walking home with their mates.
fact: A boarding school is a school where pupils live during term and go home at weekends or in the holidays; children feel all kinds of things about it.
fact: Kids from rich families often feel judged for what their parents spend, but it is not their fault.
fact: Getting lots of things doesn't make someone a bad person; gratitude, sharing and kindness matter.
fact: Children whose staff do everyday jobs may never learn to cook or clean, so it's worth asking to learn.
fact: Teasing a child for being rich is as unkind as teasing a child for being poor.
fact: Words in this lesson: «mid» means boring or average, «no cap» means no lie, «flexing» means showing off, «gutted» means very disappointed, «bruv» (London) means mate, «restroom» is American for a public toilet.
exercise: Who picks Lottie up from school every day? | Gabi, her nanny | Her dad, from work | A taxi driver | Her big sister
exercise: Why does Lottie say Gabi knows her better than Dad? | Dad works long hours and is hardly ever home | Dad doesn't love her and never has done | Gabi reads Lottie's diary every evening | Gabi has known her for twenty years
exercise: What can money not buy, in Lottie's story? | Time with Dad | A big house | A nanny | Nice biscuits
exercise: What could Lottie say to her dad? | «I miss you» | «Give me more money» | «I like Gabi more, bye» | Nothing, he's busy
exercise: «He's mid, no cap.» What is the kid saying about Monty? | He's boring, no lie | He's average height | He's in the middle | He's wearing no hat
exercise: Why was Monty gutted? | A kid only came for his pool | His pool had sprung a leak | Nobody came to his house at all | His cinema room broke down
exercise: Clementine smiled on the video call. How did she really feel? | Sad that they missed her play | Glad they missed her play | Cross about the flowers | Happy they went on their work trip
exercise: «Stop flexing.» What does «flexing» mean? | Showing off | Stretching your arms | Exercising | Sulking
exercise: Did Anya mean to show off about skiing? | No, she didn't realise how different it sounded | Yes, she wanted everybody to feel jealous | Yes, she talks about Switzerland all day | No, she's never been skiing in her life
exercise: Islay said «must be nice» about Sienna's iPhone. Who is to blame for the awkward feeling? | Neither of them | Sienna, for the phone | Islay, for saying it | Sienna's parents
exercise: Kids ask if Arnav is «a prince». What does Arnav want? | To walk home like everyone else | To be driven in an even bigger car | To be treated as a real prince | To never come to school again
exercise: «Must have cost a fortune,» a mum says about Imogen's party. What's true? | It's not Imogen's fault | Imogen should cancel it | Imogen paid for it herself | The mum is paying for it
exercise: What is the best thing Imogen can do at her party? | Be a kind host | Hide in her room | Send everyone home | Tell the mum off
exercise: What is a boarding school? | A school where you live in term time | A school that teaches you to surf | A school for children who are bored | A school with no classrooms, just boats
exercise: Henry is scared about boarding school. What's the wise move? | Tell his parents and ask questions | Pack his dog in his suitcase | Pretend he's fine and say nothing to anyone | Run away to his gran's house
exercise: Why couldn't Leo-Ray make toast? | Staff always made it at home | He's allergic to all bread | He had never seen a toaster before | He did it badly on purpose
exercise: «You're spoilt, bruv.» What does the lesson say matters more than how much stuff you get? | Saying thank you, sharing and being kind | Getting even more stuff than the others | Hiding all your stuff from your friends | Giving all your things away to the bully
test: «rich kids got nannies cos their parents don't love them». True? | No, nannies help busy parents who love their kids | Yes, a nanny means parents don't care at all | Yes, rich parents never want to see children | No, nannies are only for children who are ill
test: Monty wonders who his real friends are. What's a fair way to find out? | Notice who's around when there's no pool | Pay his friends money to keep coming over every day | Build a second, even bigger pool | Ban everyone from his house forever
test: In a film, the rich kid is mean and snobby and gets what he deserves. What's more real? | Rich kids are like any kids, kind or not | Every rich kid is mean and snobby | Rich kids never have any feelings | Only poor kids can be kind people
test: Sienna: «It's not my fault». Islay: «never said it was, mate». What could help them most? | An honest chat about how it feels | Sienna giving Islay her new phone | Islay never talking to her again | Sienna hiding her phone at school
test: What would probably happen if Clementine never told her parents how much the play mattered? | They might keep missing things without knowing | They would read her mind and come next time | They would cancel every work trip for ever after that | Nothing, parents always know how kids feel
test: Felix gets 10 presents at Christmas, says thank you for each and shares his new game. His mate still says «spoilt». What's fair? | Felix is being grateful and kind | Felix is spoilt because of the number | Felix should give all ten presents away | Felix should never say thank you again
test: Henry will board Monday to Friday and come home at weekends. A term is 12 weeks. How many weekends at home in a term? | 12 | 6 | 24 | 4
test: Leo-Ray burnt the toast and everyone laughed. What's the smartest move? | Laugh too and ask to be shown how | Storm off and phone his driver | Say toast is for poor people | Never go to a sleepover again
test: Anya's friend asks, «Did you go anywhere at half-term?» and Anya went to France. What's a kind way to share? | Say where she went, then ask about her friend's | Say nothing at all, holidays are secret | Talk about France for the rest of the day | Lie that she stayed home all week long
test: «Arnav's driver's well sus, bet he's a gangster's kid». («Sus» means suspicious.) What's fair to think? | A driver just means his parents are rich | A driver proves his family must be criminals | A driver means he's royalty from abroad | A driver means Arnav can't walk at all
test: «Gabi's with me 3 till 7, then Mum till bed at half seven, and Dad gets in at 8, no wait, half 8.» Who doesn't Lottie see on a school-day evening? | Dad | Gabi | Mum | Gabi and Mum
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relation between wealth and a parent's time? | Wealth may purchase help but not the parent's presence | Wealth guarantees a parent's constant presence at home | Wealth renders a parent's presence wholly unnecessary | Wealth obliges a parent to be absent on every occasion
exam: Identify the principal characteristic of a boarding school. | Pupils reside there during the term | Pupils attend it only at weekends | Pupils are taught there entirely online | Pupils are admitted only after the age of sixteen
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning children of wealthy families. | They have feelings as other children do, and are not to blame for their parents' spending | They are without feelings and may be teased without consequence | They are invariably arrogant and deserve whatever teasing they receive | They are responsible for their parents' expenditure upon parties
exam: Given that a pupil is told that she is «flexing» when mentioning a foreign holiday, ascertain the most appropriate conduct. | To share briefly and ask about others' holidays | To cease speaking to the classmate entirely | To describe the holiday at still greater length | To deny that any such holiday ever took place
